  "preview": "Two quadrilaterals with side lengths in algebraic expressions. For what value of x do they have the same perimeter?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Build and simplify algebraic expressions in a geometric context, evaluate them, and reason backward to find when two expressions are equal.",
    "studentPrompt": "Two quadrilaterals are shown.\n\nQuadrilateral 1 has sides: 3x, 4x, 10, and 2(-3x + 24).\n\nQuadrilateral 2 has sides: 3x, 2x, 11, and 4(6x - 21).\n\n1. If x = 4, find the perimeter of each quadrilateral. Which has the greater perimeter?\n2. Now treat x as unknown. Write a simplified expression for the perimeter of each.\n3. For what value of x do the two quadrilaterals have the same perimeter?\n4. For what value of x does Quadrilateral 1 have a perimeter of 50?",
    "image": {
      "kind": "url",
      "url": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/9582659/pexels-photo-9582659.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
      "alt": "Closeup view of a colorful patchwork quilt featuring various fabrics and patterns.",
      "credit": "Photo by Erik Mclean on Pexels"
    },
    "launch": "Show the figures. Tell groups: 'These quadrilaterals look different, but they might have the same perimeter for some x. Find that x.' Random groups, vertical whiteboards.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "expressions",
      "combining-like-terms",
      "distributive-property",
      "evaluating",
      "equations"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Layered task. Q1 is plug-and-chug (low floor). Q2 demands distribution + simplification. Q3 forces an equation that requires solving. Q4 reverses direction.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: Plug x = 4. Quad 1 perimeter = 12 + 16 + 10 + 2(-12 + 24) = 12 + 16 + 10 + 24 = 62. Quad 2 = 12 + 8 + 11 + 4(24 - 21) = 12 + 8 + 11 + 12 = 43. Quad 1 is greater.\n- Q2: Distribute and combine.\n  - Quad 1: 3x + 4x + 10 + (-6x + 48) = x + 58.\n  - Quad 2: 3x + 2x + 11 + (24x - 84) = 29x - 73.\n- Q3: Set x + 58 = 29x - 73. Solve: 131 = 28x, so x = 131/28 ≈ 4.68.\n- Q4: x + 58 = 50, so x = -8. (Discuss whether negative side lengths make sense — does the figure still 'exist'?)",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Forgetting to distribute the negative inside 2(-3x + 24) — getting -3x + 48 instead of -6x + 48.\n- Distributing 4(6x - 21) and forgetting to multiply BOTH terms by 4.\n- Mistaking 'same perimeter' for 'same shape' or 'same area.'\n- For Q4, ignoring whether x = -8 produces realistic side lengths.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- On Q2, which simplification gave you the most trouble — the negative distribution in Quad 1 or the four-term expansion in Quad 2?\n- For Q3, the answer wasn't a whole number. Is that a problem? Why or why not?\n- For Q4, x = -8 makes the algebraic perimeter equal to 50 — but plug it in. Do all four side lengths come out positive? Should we accept the answer?",
    "extensions": "- Find the value of x for which Quadrilateral 2 has perimeter 100.\n- Suppose a third quadrilateral has perimeter expression 50 + 5x. For what value of x does it match Quadrilateral 1's perimeter?\n- Construct your own pair of quadrilaterals — using algebraic side expressions — that have the same perimeter at x = 6 but never again. Is that possible?",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. At x = 4: Quad 1 = 62, Quad 2 = 43. Quad 1 has the greater perimeter.\n2. Quad 1 perimeter = x + 58. Quad 2 perimeter = 29x - 73.\n3. x + 58 = 29x - 73 → 131 = 28x → x = 131/28 ≈ 4.68. (Or 4 19/28.)\n4. x + 58 = 50 → x = -8. Side lengths plug back to: 3(-8) = -24, 4(-8) = -32, 10, and 2(-3(-8) + 24) = 2(48) = 96. Negative lengths don't form a real figure — algebraically the equation works, but geometrically x must be > 0.",
